   "": {
    "actClause": "AN ACT to amend the transportation law, in relation to requiring information\nconcerning services for human trafficking victims to be made available in\nlactation rooms in commercial service airports",
    "basePrintNo": "S6214",
    "basePrintNoStr": "S6214-2023",
    "coSponsors": {
     "items": [],
     "size": 0
    },
    "fullText": "",
    "fullTextFormats": [],
    "fullTextHtml": "",
    "fullTextTemplate": "",
    "lawCode": "Amd §14-n, Transp L",
    "lawSection": "Transportation Law",
    "memo": " \nBILL NUMBER: S6214\n\nSPONSOR: WEBB\n \nTITLE OF BILL:\n\nAn act to amend the transportation law, in relation to requiring infor-\nmation concerning services for human trafficking victims to be made\navailable in lactation rooms in commercial service airports\n\n \nPURPOSE:\n\nTo require airports to provide information concerning services for human\ntrafficking victims in lactation rooms.\n\n \nSUMMARY OF PROVISIONS:\n\nSection 1 amends section 14-n to the transportation law to require\ncommercial service airports and general aviation airports to make infor-\nmational cards or signs available in any lactation rooms, which contain\ninformation on services for trafficking victims, including the national\nhuman trafficking hotline number.\n\nSection 2 provides the effective date.\n\n \nJUSTIFICATION:\n\nHuman trafficking is a global phenomenon that generates $150 billion in\nannual profits. In 2021, an estimated 25 million people were trafficked.\nThe convenience and anonymity of airports makes it convenient for traf-\nfickers to move victims.\n\nBecause airports are known hubs used to traffic victims both interna-\ntionally and domestically, they are an ideal location to provide infor-\nmation about victim services. This information can not only inform\nvictims, but also other travelers about the availability of services.\nMany victims are heavily supervised and controlled while in transit,\npreventing them from safely seeking help or finding resources without\nfear of retaliation or threat.\n\nChapter 389 of 2022 required commercial service and general aviation\nairports to visibly post human trafficking informational signs or cards\nin its public restrooms. This legislation would require the signs or\ncards to also be posted in any lactation rooms. Through this legis-\nlation, airports can assume a more prominent role in responding to and\npreventing human trafficking. It will also afford victims the tools and\nresources to hopefully escape their captors.\n\n \nLEGISLATIVE HISTORY:\n\n2022: A. 10685\n\n \nFISCAL IMPLICATIONS:\n\nNone.\n\n \nEFFECTIVE DATE:\nThis act shall take effect on the same date and in the same manner as\nchapter 389 of 2022 takes effect.\n",
